  "title": "Match packets against an OpenFlow flow table",
  "estimatedMinutes": 25,
  "instructions": "## Be the switch\n\nAn OpenFlow switch holds a **flow table** installed by the controller. Each entry has a priority, match fields and actions. For each packet the switch picks the **highest-priority** entry whose fields all match. A field missing from an entry is a wildcard. If nothing matches, the **table-miss** behaviour sends the packet to the controller as a packet-in.\n\nThe campus table in this lab:\n\n| Priority | Match | Actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 | ipv4_dst 10.0.2.0/24, tcp_dst 22 | DROP |\n| 150 | ipv4_dst 10.0.2.0/24 | OUTPUT:2 |\n| 100 | eth_type arp | FLOOD |\n| 50 | ipv4_dst 10.0.0.0/16 | OUTPUT:3 |\n| 10 | in_port 1 | OUTPUT:4 |\n\n## Do this\n\n- `ipInPrefix(ip, prefix)`: true when the dotted address is inside the CIDR prefix.\n- `matchFlow(table, packet)`: the actions of the winning entry, or `[\"CONTROLLER\"]` on a table miss. An entry with ipv4_dst never matches a packet that has no ipv4_dst, such as an ARP packet.\n\nEntries look like `{ priority, match: { ipv4_dst, tcp_dst, eth_type, in_port }, actions: [...] }`. Packets carry the same field names.",
  "config": {
    "language": "javascript"
  },
